1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,544
Skip the coordinator fantasy for a moment
and book a trip the way product actually
sells it: flight seat, hotel night, rental
car. Three services. Three local
databases. A card charge at the end.

2
00:00:14,663 --> 00:00:21,527
You cannot hold XA locks across a hotel
API that takes eight seconds and a car
broker that times out on Tuesdays.

3
00:00:21,647 --> 00:00:35,423
You need a different promise — each step
commits locally, and if a later step
fails, earlier steps run compensating
actions that undo their business effect.
That is the saga pattern. Not one global
transaction.

4
00:00:35,543 --> 00:00:51,191
A narrative of local transactions with
explicit recovery. Step one: reserve the
flight seat — local commit in inventory.
Step two: hold the hotel room — local
commit in hotels. Step three: reserve the
car.

5
00:00:51,311 --> 00:00:59,447
Step four: charge the card. If the charge
fails, you do not “roll back” the seat the
way JDBC rolls back a row.

6
00:00:59,567 --> 00:01:10,584
You call releaseSeat, cancelHotelHold, and
releaseCar — compensations that are
themselves ordinary local transactions.
Guests may briefly see a held seat that
later frees.

7
00:01:10,703 --> 00:01:25,031
Saga trades immediate global consistency
for availability and clearer failure
handling across services. Look at this
java for a moment. That sketch is
orchestration: one component directs steps
and compensations.

8
00:01:25,151 --> 00:01:39,431
Choreography is the other style — each
service listens for events and reacts.
SeatReserved triggers hotel holding.
HotelHeld triggers car reservation.
PaymentFailed triggers releases and
cancels.

9
00:01:39,551 --> 00:01:51,095
Orchestration is easier to follow in one
place. Choreography avoids a central boss
but scatters the flow. Both are sagas if
they share the compensation mindset.

10
00:01:51,215 --> 00:01:57,071
Spring does not ship a single @Saga
annotation that finishes distributed
workflows for you.

11
00:01:57,191 --> 00:02:08,735
Building blocks yes: local @Transactional
per service, messaging, transactional
outbox so an event publish reliably
follows a local commit, and application
code or state machines that track
progress.

12
00:02:08,855 --> 00:02:22,967
Libraries exist on top. The idea you must
own is independent of any one of them:
forward actions plus compensations,
idempotent handlers, and timeouts.
Idempotency matters because retries
duplicate.

13
00:02:23,087 --> 00:02:36,527
Releasing an already-released seat must be
safe. Payments need clear capture versus
void semantics. Persist saga state — which
steps succeeded — so a crash mid-flow can
resume or compensate without guessing.

14
00:02:36,647 --> 00:02:45,551
Kill early misconceptions. Saga is not XA
with friendlier branding; it deliberately
allows temporary inconsistency.

15
00:02:45,671 --> 00:02:54,120
Compensation is not always the mechanical
inverse of insert — canceling a started
trip may mean refund plus restock, not
deleting history.

16
00:02:54,240 --> 00:03:00,840
And saga does not remove local
transactions; every step still wants a
solid boundary inside its service.

17
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:08,640
We have closed the transaction arc from
one-method boundaries through nesting,
isolation, rollback rules, XA limits, and
sagas.

18
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:20,208
Step back and notice what kept
intercepting method calls: begin
transaction, maybe later log, authorize,
or time. That cross-cutting interception
is not unique to transactions.

19
00:03:20,328 --> 00:03:28,368
What modularizes those concerns so every
harbor service method does not copy-paste
them? Aspect-oriented programming.
